: proto -- the RealXV6 filesystem this image carries on rk0, in mkfs format:
: what is on the disk, and where each file comes from.  An image hands
: someone a set of programs, and which programs those are is a decision, so
: this list is written rather than arrived at: a name here is a file that
: ships, a name absent is a file withheld.
:
: /bin is not carried here, it is built.  Every program is named through
: $BIN, the directory MKBIN fills -- see the legend at the foot -- so what
: ships for a name here is that program's SOURCE and the reader compiles
: the a.out.  MKBIN's list and this one name exactly the same programs.
:
: /dev and /bin describe the kernel this image actually runs on, the one on
: the boot floppy beside it: it has a floppy driver, so fd0 and fd1 are here
: and fdtest with them, and reboot(2) is slot 49 of its sysent, so /bin has
: reboot.
:
: No binary is named below at all: a /bin line names what MKBIN compiles
: from the source beside it.  A program whose source is not in this tree
: therefore cannot appear here -- nothing would be able to build it.
:
: Block 0 stays zero -- the '-' below.  The floppy is what boots; a boot
: sector written where nothing reads it would only suggest otherwise.
:
-
4000 100 872
d--755 0 0
init    ---755 0 0 $BIN/INIT.AO
bin     d--755 0 0
        cat         ---755 0 0 $BIN/CAT.AO
        chmod       ---755 0 0 $BIN/CHMOD.AO
        cp          ---755 0 0 $BIN/CP.AO
        date        ---755 0 0 $BIN/DATE.AO
        echo        ---755 0 0 $BIN/ECHO.AO
        ed          ---755 0 0 $BIN/ED.AO
        fib         ---755 0 0 $BIN/FIB.AO
        forktest    ---755 0 0 $BIN/FORKTEST.AO
        grep        ---755 0 0 $BIN/GREP.AO
        id          ---755 0 0 $BIN/ID.AO
        kill        ---755 0 0 $BIN/KILL.AO
        ln          ---755 0 0 $BIN/LN.AO
        login       ---755 0 0 $BIN/LOGIN.AO
        ls          ---755 0 0 $BIN/LS.AO
        mkdir       ---755 0 0 $BIN/MKDIR.AO
        mkfs        ---755 0 0 $BIN/MKFS.AO
        mknod       ---755 0 0 $BIN/MKNOD.AO
        more        ---755 0 0 $BIN/MORE.AO
        mount       ---755 0 0 $BIN/MOUNT.AO
        nice        ---755 0 0 $BIN/NICE.AO
        ps          ---755 0 0 $BIN/PS.AO
        pwd         ---755 0 0 $BIN/PWD.AO
        rm          ---755 0 0 $BIN/RM.AO
        sh          ---755 0 0 $BIN/SH.AO
        sigtest     ---755 0 0 $BIN/SIGTEST.AO
        sleep       ---755 0 0 $BIN/SLEEP.AO
        stty        ---755 0 0 $BIN/STTY.AO
        sync        ---755 0 0 $BIN/SYNC.AO
        umount      ---755 0 0 $BIN/UMOUNT.AO
        wc          ---755 0 0 $BIN/WC.AO
        c88         ---755 0 0 $BIN/C88.AO
        gen         ---755 0 0 $BIN/GEN.AO
        asm88       ---755 0 0 $BIN/ASM88.AO
        dbind       ---755 0 0 $BIN/DBIND.AO
        dcmp        ---755 0 0 $BIN/DCMP.AO
        dlib88      ---755 0 0 $BIN/DLIB88.AO
        drun        ---755 0 0 $BIN/DRUN.AO
        d2a         ---755 0 0 $BIN/D2A.AO
        e2c         ---755 0 0 $BIN/E2C.AO
        mkboot      ---755 0 0 $BIN/MKBOOT.AO
        reboot      ---755 0 0 $BIN/REBOOT.AO
        fdtest      ---755 0 0 $BIN/FDTEST.AO
        hello       ---755 0 0 $BIN/HELLO.AO
        zombie      ---755 0 0 $BIN/ZOMBIE.AO
        $
dev     d--755 0 0
        console c--666 0 0 0 0
        rk0     b--644 0 0 0 0
        rk1     b--644 0 0 0 1
        rk2     b--644 0 0 0 2
        rk3     b--644 0 0 0 3
        rrk0    c--644 0 0 2 0
        rrk1    c--644 0 0 2 1
        rrk2    c--644 0 0 2 2
        rrk3    c--644 0 0 2 3
        fd0     b--644 0 0 1 0
        fd1     b--644 0 0 1 1
        mem     c--644 0 0 1 0
        kmem    c--644 0 0 1 1
        null    c--644 0 0 1 2
        $
etc     d--755 0 0
$
: /test -- two goldens, and nothing else.  Both are read by the on-target
: build scripts themselves: mkkern ends by dcmp'ing the kernel it just
: built against REFK.COM, and mkimg checks its floppy layout against
: REFF.IMG.  Withhold them and the two scripts this stage exists for stop
: at their last step.  Neither is carried: $KBIN is what MKKBIN builds on
: the reader's DOS, the way $BIN is what MKBIN builds.
test    d--755 0 0
        REFK.COM    ---644 0 0 $KBIN/REFK.COM
        REFF.IMG    ---644 0 0 $KBIN/REFF.IMG
        $
tmp     d--777 0 0
$
usr     d--755 0 0
        source  d--755 0 0
        $
$
: /src is the MOUNT POINT for rk1, which carries the on-target source
: tree (see proto.rk1).  It must stay an empty directory here.
src     d--777 0 0
        $
$

: mkfs proto file format
: boot sector (block 0)
: filsys_blks inode_blks swap_blks
: root_dir
: input_lines...
: $
:
: input_line
:   name modestr uid gid external_path
: modestr (ex: d--755 or ---666)
: ---     file
: b--     block
: c--     char
: d--     directory (adds next lines to directory until '$')
:  u      setuid
:   g     setgid
:    666  mode
:
: A ':' at the start of a word comments out the rest of the line.
:
: external_path is relative to this file's directory, or begins with a $NAME
: token the caller defines on the mkfs command line (-D NAME=PATH):
:   $BUILD   drive_c/BUILD        -- compiler component sources
:   $RT      drive_c/XV6/RT       -- V6 runtime sources
:   $KERNEL  drive_c/XV6/KERNEL   -- RealXV6 kernel sources (KEN/ DMR/ H/)
:   $BIN     drive_c/XV6/BIN      -- the a.out MKBIN builds, one per /bin
:                                 program (run CROSS, then MKBIN, first)
:   $KBIN    drive_c/XV6/KBIN     -- the kernel, the boot sector and the
:                                 layout golden MKKBIN builds (after MKBIN)
